//!
//! # Mm
//!
//! Memory management: physical frames, the kernel page tables, the kernel
//! heap, per-process address spaces and the Limine modules.
//!
//! ## Public API
//!
//! | Name                | File               | Purpose                                                  |
//! |---------------------|--------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
//! | `Mm::Pmm`           | `pmm.ign`          | bitmap allocator of 4 KiB physical frames                |
//! | `Mm::Vmm`           | `vmm.ign`          | the kernel page tables, `map`, `unmap`, `translate`      |
//! | `Mm::AddressSpace`  | `address_space.ign`| private user halves and checked copies to and from user memory |
//! | `Mm::Heap`          | `heap.ign`         | kernel heap behind `@allocHandler` and `@freeHandler`    |
//! | `Mm::Modules`       | `modules.ign`      | the files Limine loaded next to the kernel               |
//!
//! ## Dependencies
//!
//! | Imports   | Why                                                                |
//! |-----------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|
//! | `Boot`    | the memory map, the direct map, the executable address             |
//! | `Arch`    | control registers, `invlpg`, interrupt flag handling               |
//! | `Lib`     | `memcpy`, `memset`                                                 |
//! | `Drivers` | `Mm::Modules` lists each module on the console                     |
//!
//! `Mm::Modules` is here and not in `Boot` because it maps the module pages
//! with `Mm::Vmm::mapRange`, and `Mm` already imports `Boot`.
//!
//! The order of the boot calls is fixed: `Mm::Pmm::initialize`, then
//! `Mm::Vmm::initialize` (which takes its page-table frames from the PMM),
//! then `Mm::Modules::initialize` and `Mm::Heap::initialize`, which map their
//! pages through the VMM.

import _ from "./address_space";
import _ from "./heap";
import _ from "./modules";
import _ from "./pmm";
import _ from "./vmm";

/// Aggregate namespace of the memory manager. The files above contribute the
/// sub-namespaces listed in the module header.
export namespace Mm {}
